  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 223 AM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nA cold front swings through Wednesday night but is quickly\nfollowed by a warm front that moves in Thursday night. The cold\nfront will be rather weak, and will mostly be a wind shift with\nlittle relief in the warm temperatures. High temperatures will\nbe in the upper 80s for the rest of the work week with clear\nskies and no rain expected. Lows will continue to sit in the\nupper 50s to lower 60s range. \n\nThe first chance of rain makes an appearance later this weekend\nas the remnants of newly named Tropical Storm Isaias move into\nthe area. The low pressure system is forecast to move north\nthrough eastern LA/AR and could clip the eastern Ozarks Saturday\nnight into Sunday. It won't bring widespread rainfall, and areas\nwest of highway 63 will likely stay dry. Right now we have a\n30-50% chance of rain for the eastern Ozarks for Saturday night\nwith rain dissipating by Sunday morning. The track of the system\nwill determine where the rain will fall. Though, models are\nmostly in agreement that the building ridge over the Central\nPlains this weekend will keep the remnants east and push it\ntowards the Ohio River Valley. This means it'll be more likely\nfor the system to go further east than shift back towards the\nwest. \n\nHighs through the weekend and into the start of next week will \nsit in the mid 80s once again as the high pressure continues to \nbuild and stay put over the Central Plains into the start of \nnext week.",
